    "body": "## Line and headline (D21, docs/labs/session-goal.md)\n\nLine: no model line - tooling · count 0/3 · headline 37/232 → 37/232 (unchanged)\n\n**D30 check-port: `mistakes.guard_covered` — the rule I removed, done right.** Tooling only.\n\nDecision: **KEEP** (tooling).\n\n## Agreement\n\n| rule | severity | Python | Rust |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `mistakes.guard_covered` | warn | all pass | 0 findings |\n\nCoverage reads **33 of 40 rules. Seven remain.**\n\n## The history: I built this, measured it, found it wrong, and removed it\n\nTwo units ago I implemented this rule against **`ruleinventory::all_ids()` — the rules the *port* has implemented** — while the rule must resolve against the rules the **engine** registers. Four real lessons whose guards name a `prlint.*` gate were reported as *\"not a rule this engine registers.\"* **Rust said 4, Python said 0.**\n\n**I removed the function rather than ship it approximate**, because *\"a guard check against an incomplete port is not a weaker check, it is a wrong one — it would tell authors to delete guards on real rules.\"*\n\n## And the fix repeated the mistake once\n\nMy first correction put the id set in a data file, generated from **`checkrules.rules()` — 40 ids.** **The probe still read 4 findings.**\n\n**Measured against Python: `_known_rule_ids()` returns 53.** The engine resolves guards from **three sources**:\n\n| source | contribution |\n|---|---|\n| `checkrules.rules()` | 40 |\n| each module's own `register_rules()` | 13 more |\n| `prlint.GATE_RULES` | gates raised against a **PR body**, not repo state |\n\n**My data file took the first source and stopped — the same false positive, in a new place.**\n\n**The lesson repeated exactly: I asked \"what rules are registered\" and answered with the first list I found, twice, instead of reading the function that answers the question.** The second time I caught it **only because the same probe that caught the first still read 4.**\n\n## Why the extra 13 are not padding\n\n**`prlint`'s gates are raised against a PR body, which the engine never has** — so they are declared as data rather than registered as checks. **A lesson may still cite one as its guard, and four do.** **Resolving guards from the check registry alone excludes precisely the guards a lesson is most likely to name, because the lessons are about how PRs are written.**\n\n```\ncargo fmt --check                               -> clean\ncargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings       -> 0 findings\ncargo test -p uor-r4-harness                    -> 316 passed (5 new)\npython3 -m unittest discover -s tests/harness   -> 319 passed\nparity.py                                       -> exit 0, rule inventory: 33 implemented\n```\n\nRecord: `docs/labs/port-check-guard-covered-2026-10-13/README.md`\n\n## Checklist\n\n- [x] Checks run at this exact head (above)\n- [x] Tooling only; no capability or headline changed\n- [x] Record + current-state entry carried\n- [x] Branch deleted after merge\n\n### Stated rather than hidden\n\n- **`check` still delegates.** Seven rules remain.\n- **The id set is a snapshot refreshed by hand.** A rule added to the engine and not to this file would make Rust report **a valid guard as unknown — a finding that is wrong rather than missing.** A test asserts the count so a drift fails loudly, **but nothing refreshes the file automatically.**\n- **The file was generated by running Python's own `_known_rule_ids()`.** That is the right source and also a **one-way dependency**: when Python is retired this must be maintained by hand, or regenerated from Rust once Rust owns the rule registry.\n- **Only `block` severity is checked**, matching Python — a `warn` lesson with a nonsense guard passes in both.\n\nReferences #2028\n",
